WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.002
Use-after-free prevention is not only a
compiler feature.

2
00:00:03.082 --> 00:00:04.484
It is also an API design practice.

3
00:00:04.564 --> 00:00:07.316
A good API communicates whether the caller
transfers ownership,

4
00:00:07.396 --> 00:00:09.248
borrows temporarily, receives owned data,

5
00:00:09.328 --> 00:00:11.430
or receives a reference tied to an
existing owner.

6
00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:13.512
The signature should tell the lifecycle
story.

7
00:00:13.592 --> 00:00:16.745
Owned returns are often safer when there
is no durable owner to borrow from.

8
00:00:16.825 --> 00:00:18.877
If a function builds a result, normalizes
input,

9
00:00:18.957 --> 00:00:21.659
or extracts a value that needs to survive
beyond the input scope,

10
00:00:21.739 --> 00:00:25.293
returning an owned value can be clearer
than forcing a borrowed relationship that is

11
00:00:25.373 --> 00:00:26.073
hard to satisfy.

12
00:00:26.153 --> 00:00:28.355
Encapsulation protects internal state
lifetimes.

13
00:00:28.435 --> 00:00:31.738
A type can own its resources and expose
methods that do not leak references into

14
00:00:31.818 --> 00:00:32.869
short-lived internals.

15
00:00:32.949 --> 00:00:36.352
Callers interact with stable operations
instead of depending on private buffer

16
00:00:36.432 --> 00:00:38.034
locations, temporary parsing state,

17
00:00:38.114 --> 00:00:40.166
or internal handles that may be cleaned up
later.

18
00:00:40.246 --> 00:00:43.049
Constructors and domain types can assemble
validated owned data.

19
00:00:43.129 --> 00:00:46.332
Instead of passing around loosely related
handles, flags, and references,

20
00:00:46.412 --> 00:00:49.715
an API can validate once and then expose a
type that represents a valid resource

21
00:00:49.795 --> 00:00:50.095
state.

22
00:00:50.175 --> 00:00:52.978
This reduces repeated lifetime assumptions
across the codebase.

23
00:00:53.058 --> 00:00:54.409
The safe path should be obvious.

24
00:00:54.489 --> 00:00:57.592
If callers can accidentally store a
borrowed reference beyond the owner,

25
00:00:57.672 --> 00:01:00.374
retain a callback after cleanup, or use a
handle after shutdown,

26
00:01:00.454 --> 00:01:02.056
the API is asking too much of its users.

27
00:01:02.136 --> 00:01:05.839
Defensive API design makes invalid
lifetime relationships difficult or impossible to

28
00:01:05.919 --> 00:01:06.820
express in safe code.
